Here's an example of how you can use a `LinkedList` to implement a stack:

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class Stack {
    private LinkedList<Integer> stack;

    public Stack() {
        stack = new LinkedList<>();
    }

    public void push(int element) {
        stack.addLast(element);
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (!isEmpty()) {
            return stack.removeLast();
        } else {
            return -1; // or throw an exception
        }
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }
}
```

In this example, the `push` method adds an element to the end of the list, and the `pop` method removes an element from the end of the list. The `isEmpty` method checks if the stack is empty.

